/images/RRA/tutorial/image009.jpg","alt","Guidance Icon",0,"v","shapes","Picture_x0020_23"],["src","assets/images/RRA/tutorial/image011.jpg","alt","Comments Icon",0,"v","shapes","Picture_x0020_22"],["src","assets/images/RRA/tutorial/image013.jpg","alt","Artifacts References Icon",0,"v","shapes","Picture_x0020_21"],["src","assets/images/RRA/tutorial/image014.png","alt","Tools",2,"height","2rem"],["src","assets/images/RRA/tutorial/image015.jpg","alt","References Icon",0,"v","shapes","Picture_x0020_20"],["src","assets/images/RRA/tutorial/image016.jpg","alt","Observations Icon",0,"v","shapes","Picture_x0020_19"],["src","assets/images/RRA/tutorial/image018.jpg","alt","Feedback Icon",0,"v","shapes","Picture_x0020_18"],[3,"page"]],template:function(n,t){1&n&&(aa(0,"div",0),aa(1,"h3",1),Qa(2,"Ransomware Readiness Tutorial"),ca(),aa(3,"div"),aa(4,"p"),Qa(5,"Ransomware is an ever-evolving form of malware designed to encrypt files on a device, rendering any files and the systems that rely on them unusable. Malicious actors then demand ransom in exchange for decryption. Ransomware actors often target and threaten to sell or leak exfiltrated data or authentication information if the ransom is not paid. In recent years, ransomware incidents have become increasingly prevalent among the Nation\u2019s state, local, tribal, and territorial (SLTT) government entities and critical infrastructure organizations."),ca(),aa(6,"p"),Qa(7,"Malicious actors continue to adjust and evolve their ransomware tactics over time, and CISA analysts remain vigilant in maintaining awareness of ransomware attacks and associated tactics, techniques, and procedures across the country and around the world."),ca(),aa(8,"p"),Qa(9,"This Ransomware Readiness Assessment (RRA) will help you understand your cybersecurity posture with respect to the ever-evolving threat of ransomware. The RRA also provides a clear path for improvement, and contains an evolving progression of questions tiered by the categories of basic, intermediate, and advanced. This is intended to help an organization improve by focusing on the basics first, and then progressing by implementing practices through the intermediate and advanced categories."),aa(10,"span"),Qa(11,"\xa0 "),ca(),ca(),aa(12,"p"),aa(13,"b"),Qa(14,"Looking to learn more about this growing cyber threat? "),aa(15,"span"),Qa(16,"\xa0"),ca(),ca(),ca(),aa(17,"p"),aa(18,"span"),Qa(19,"The NEW Ransomware Guide is a great place to start. The Guide, released in September 2020, represents a joint effort between CISA and the Multi-State Information Sharing and Analysis Center (MS-ISAC). The"),ca(),aa(20,"span"),Qa(21,"\xa0"),ca(),aa(22,"span"),aa(23,"a",2),aa(24,"span"),Qa(25,"joint Ransomware Guide"),ca(),ca(),ca(),aa(26,"span"),Qa(27,"\xa0"),ca(),aa(28,"span"),Qa(29,"includes industry best practices and a response checklist that can serve as a ransomware-specific addendum to organization cyber incident response plans.\xa0 "),ca(),ca(),aa(30,"p"),aa(31,"span"),Qa(32,"In January 2021, CISA\xa0unveiled the\xa0"),ca(),aa(33,"span"),aa(34,"a",3),aa(35,"span"),Qa(36,"Reduce the Risk of Ransomware Campaign"),ca(),ca(),ca(),aa(37,"span"),Qa(38,"\xa0to raise awareness and instigate actions to combat this\xa0ongoing and evolving threat. The campaign is a focused, coordinated and sustained effort to encourage public and private sector organizations to implement best practices, tools and resources that can help them mitigate ransomware risk. "),ca(),ca(),aa(39,"p"),aa(40,"span"),Qa(41,"For critical infrastructure owners and operators of industrial control systems, CISA published a Fact Sheet,\xa0"),ca(),aa(42,"span"),aa(43,"a",4),aa(44,"span"),Qa(45,"Rising Ransomware Threat to Operational Technology Assets"),ca(),ca(),ca(),aa(46,"span"),Qa(47,", that provides information on the rising risk of ransomware to ICS and recommended actions to reduce the risk of becoming a victim as well as severe business or functional degradation should they fall victim. "),ca(),ca(),aa(48,"p"),aa(49,"span"),Qa(50,"CISA also continues to publish cybersecurity alerts and tips, to include those specific to ransomware. Visit "),ca(),aa(51,"span"),aa(52,"a",5),aa(53,"span"),Qa(54,"Ransomware Alerts and Tips | CISA"),ca(),ca(),ca(),aa(55,"span"),Qa(56," and click subscribe. "),ca(),ca(),aa(57,"span"),la(58,"br",6),ca(),aa(59,"p"),aa(60,"b"),aa(61,"span"),Qa(62,"Ransomware Readiness Assessment Composition: Goals and Practice Questions "),ca(),ca(),ca(),aa(63,"p"),Qa(64,"This assessment represents one Domain that addresses Ransomware and is constructed with ten Goals and Practice Questions that address each Goal."),ca(),aa(65,"p"),Qa(66,"The Practice Questions are tiered as Basic, Intermediate, and Advanced. Each question contains an identifier structured as follows: "),ca(),aa(67,"p"),aa(68,"b"),Qa(69,"Goal"),ca(),Qa(70," [GG] : "),aa(71,"b"),Qa(72,"Level"),ca(),Qa(73," [B,I,A] . "),aa(74,"b"),Qa(75,"Question Number"),ca(),Qa(76," [Q##] "),ca(),aa(77,"p"),Qa(78,"An example of the above structure is "),aa(79,"b"),Qa(80,"AM:I.Q03"),ca(),Qa(81,", which represents "),aa(82,"b"),Qa(83,"Asset Management, Level \u201cIntermediate,\u201d Question Number 3. "),ca(),ca(),aa(84,"p"),Qa(85,"The blue oval beneath each identifier indicates the Basic, Intermediate, or Advanced tier for the question. "),ca(),aa(86,"p"),aa(87,"span"),la(88,"img",7),ca(),ca(),aa(89,"p"),Qa(90,"The RRA practices and tiers (basic, intermediate, and advanced) are designed to:"),ca(),aa(91,"ul"),aa(92,"li"),Qa(93," assist an organization to understand its current state of readiness to address the threat of ransomware "),ca(),aa(94,"li"),Qa(95," to assist the organization with implementing a focused path for improvement. "),ca(),ca(),aa(96,"p"),aa(97,"b"),Qa(98,"Implementation approach:"),ca(),ca(),aa(99,"ul"),aa(100,"li"),Qa(101,"Organizations that are unfamiliar with ransomware and need to begin basic cyber hygiene safeguards against ransomware threats should focus on implementing the basic tier of practices."),ca(),aa(102,"li"),Qa(103,"After completing the basic tier of the improvement effort, organizations looking to further improve their risk posture against ransomware should implement the intermediate tier of practices."),ca(),aa(104,"li"),Qa(105,"Organizations that have already adopted basic and intermediate level RRA practices and are looking to improve their response to ransomware threats should focus on adopting the practices in the Advanced Tier."),ca(),ca(),aa(106,"p"),Qa(107,"This suggested path to improvement can always be adapted by the organization to suit its own needs."),aa(108,"span"),Qa(109,"\xa0 "),ca(),aa(110,"span"),Qa(111,"\xa0"),ca(),ca(),aa(112,"p"),Qa(113,"Each practice question contains a yes/no response scale to evaluate whether the organization has implemented each practice:"),ca(),aa(114,"p"),aa(115,"span"),la(116,"img",8),ca(),ca(),aa(117,"ul",9),aa(118,"li"),aa(119,"b"),Qa(120,"Yes"),ca(),Qa(121,"\xa0\u2013 the organization fully performs the activity specified in the question."),ca(),aa(122,"li"),aa(123,"b"),Qa(124,"No"),ca(),Qa(125,"\xa0\u2013 the organization does not perform the activity completely. "),ca(),ca(),aa(126,"p"),Qa(127,"Practice Questions are presented in the following format:"),ca(),aa(128,"p"),aa(129,"span"),la(130,"img",10),ca(),ca(),aa(131,"p"),aa(132,"b"),aa(133,"span"),la(134,"img",11),ca(),aa(135,"span"),aa(136,"span"),Qa(137,"\xa0 "),ca(),Qa(138,"Mark for Review"),ca(),ca(),Qa(139,': To the right of the answer key is the flag icon which can be used to mark a practice for review. Marked questions are listed in the "RRA Comments and Marked for Review" report. In this section of the CSET Report Page (left side navigation \u201cReports\u201d), the user can recall any practice questions previously marked for review along with the specific comments.'),ca(),aa(140,"p"),aa(141,"span"),la(142,"img",12),ca(),aa(143,"b"),aa(144,"span"),Qa(145,"\xa0"),ca(),aa(146,"span"),Qa(147,"\xa0 "),ca(),Qa(148,"Guidance"),ca(),Qa(149,": To aid the RRA user, each question is supported by guidance.\xa0 The guidance should be referred to for background, possible examples, and for criteria that defines the requirements for a \u201cYes\u201d response.\xa0 The guidance is engaged by selecting the Guidance Icon containing the letter \u201cG\u201d located below the question."),ca(),aa(150,"p"),aa(151,"span"),la(152,"img",13),ca(),aa(153,"span"),Qa(154,"\xa0 "),ca(),aa(155,"b"),Qa(156,"Comments"),ca(),Qa(157,": Selecting this icon will display a text box that may be used to make comments regarding each question. For example, the user of an assessment may make a note for future reference as to the reasoning behind an \u201cNO\u201d response. Note: these comments are not included in the official report and are meant to be private to the facilitator."),ca(),aa(158,"p"),Qa(159,"Practices with comments are listed in the \u201cRansomware Readiness Assessment: Comments and Marked for Review\u201d report. In this section of the CSET Report Page (left side navigation \u201cReports\u201d), the user can recall any practice questions previously marked for review along with the specific comments."),ca(),aa(160,"p"),aa(161,"span"),la(162,"img",14),ca(),aa(163,"span"),Qa(164,"\xa0 "),ca(),aa(165,"b"),Qa(166,"Artifacts/Documents"),ca(),Qa(167,": Easily associate, track, and store evidence, artifacts, and other supporting documents throughout the assessment. "),ca(),aa(168,"p"),Qa(169,"These documents are stored with the assessment in the CSET Tools tab under Assessment Documents. "),aa(170,"span"),la(171,"img",15),ca(),ca(),aa(172,"p"),aa(173,"span"),la(174,"img",16),ca(),aa(175,"span"),Qa(176,"\xa0 "),ca(),aa(177,"b"),Qa(178,"References"),ca(),Qa(179,": Selecting the References Icon will display the references."),ca(),aa(180,"p"),aa(181,"span"),la(182,"img",17),ca(),aa(183,"span"),Qa(184,"\xa0 "),ca(),aa(185,"b"),Qa(186,"Observations"),ca(),Qa(187,": Selecting this icon will pop up an observati